1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The least plausible option is H. It fabricates an entirely unattested esoteric/erotic reinterpretation of the Kuji-in (nine hand seals) as “nine holes” yin-yang rituals, then claims later copyists reduced those symbols to neutral circles. No surviving ninja treatise, including the Bansenshukai itself, describes such practices, and the reduction sequence required by the theory has zero documentary support. All other choices, while varying in historical probability, at least rest on attested mechanisms (moral censorship, state secrecy, physical wear, aburidashi, oral mnemonics, etc.) rather than wholesale invention of new ritual content.

(The next-most-implausible are C and D, which founder on simple chronology: Lady Saigō died in 1589 and the Oniwaban were not created until the early eighteenth century, both well outside the 1676 compilation and subsequent copying window.)

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The question requires identifying the single least plausible explanation among the eight, based strictly on fit with known history, internal consistency of the Bansenshukai’s transmission, and the fact that every surviving copy shows exactly the same pattern of filled and empty circles.

C is the weakest by a wide margin. Lady Saigō died in 1589, nearly a century before Fujibayashi even compiled the Bansenshukai (1676). Hattori Hanzo likewise died in 1596; the “stepdaughter” claim has no documentary basis. The Oniwaban did not yet exist in any organized form that could have policed Iga-family manuscripts. Therefore the entire causal chain—kunoichi techniques used by Saigō → fear of Tokugawa legitimacy crisis → systematic redaction of a 1676 text—rests on anachronism and invented biography. All other options, however speculative, at least operate within the correct century and do not require rewriting the basic timeline of the people and institutions named in the scroll itself.}

