21.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H remains the single least plausible by any historical standard. It alone fabricates an entire, unattested esoteric system—recasting documented Kuji-in hand seals as genital “nine holes,” grafting on Taoist erotic yin-yang rituals, and chakra-style energy amplification—none of which appears in the Bansenshukai, any other Iga/Kōga manual, or Edo-period sources. The further claim that every later copyist independently recognized and uniformly reduced this nonexistent sexual-mystical layer to identical neutral circles is both mechanically implausible and unsupported by manuscript evidence.  

By contrast, the objections to C, while factually grounded on Saigō’s 1589 death date, leave open the possibility that earlier Iga-family oral or proto-written traditions (later incorporated into the 1676 compilation) could have carried politically sensitive material. Institutional memory within the Hattori network and Tokugawa court circles operated through informal channels well before the formal Oniwaban; a motive to sanitize any written trace of female operatives tied to regime-founding lore is therefore still conceivable, even if the specific biographical link remains weak. The uniform blank pattern across all surviving lineages is far more economically explained by a single early editorial decision—whether moral, institutional, or mnemonic—than by H’s wholesale invention of ritual content that contradicts the pragmatic tone of the source itself.  

If the group is seeking the explanation with the least documentary anchor, H stands apart; C, though speculative, at least operates within attested mechanisms of Tokugawa-era document control.}

